<p>The secret to a stress-free pre-Christmas clean is spreading it out. Tackle the big jobs, such as the oven, carpets and spare room, in late November or early December, then keep the final week for quick surface cleans of the kitchen, bathroom and living room. Leave it all until Christmas Eve and you'll spend the day you should be enjoying with a mop in your hand.</p>
<p>This Christmas cleaning checklist is built around that idea. If you're hosting the whole family in Hull, or putting up relatives travelling in from <a href="{{area:beverley}}">Beverley</a> or <a href="{{area:anlaby-willerby-kirk-ella}}">Willerby and Kirk Ella</a>, it covers what to clean, in which order, and when.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Start three to four weeks out with the deep jobs, so the last week is just a refresh.</li><li>Clean the oven and clear the fridge before the big food shop, not after.</li><li>Dust high surfaces and clean carpets before the decorations go up.</li><li>Keep a one-hour reset list for the day before guests arrive.</li></ul></div>
<h2>When should you start Christmas cleaning?</h2>
<p>Three to four weeks before Christmas is a sensible starting point. It gives carpets time to dry, leaves room for the oven to be cleaned before you need it, and means December weekends aren't swallowed whole by housework. Here's a simple countdown:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What to clean</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Three to four weeks before</td><td>Declutter, guest room deep clean, carpet and sofa cleaning, windows inside</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two weeks before</td><td>Oven, hob and extractor, kitchen cupboards, dust high surfaces before decorating</td></tr>
<tr><td>The week before</td><td>Fridge and freezer clear-out before the big shop, bathroom deep clean, wash guest bedding</td></tr>
<tr><td>The day before guests arrive</td><td>Quick reset: surfaces, floors, toilet, fresh towels, empty bins</td></tr>
<tr><td>Between Christmas and New Year</td><td>Post-party clean-up, spot-clean carpets, wipe down the oven after the roast</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If time is tight, a <a href="{{service:one-off-cleaning}}">one-off clean</a> in early December takes care of the heavy lifting, leaving you with light top-ups.</p>
<h2>Guest room checklist: bedding, storage and fresh air</h2>
<p>Spare rooms are often used as storage for eleven months of the year, so they usually need the most attention.</p>
<ul>
<li>Clear the clutter and make space in a drawer and on the rail, with a few empty hangers.</li>
<li>Dust from the top down: light shade, curtain pole, tops of wardrobes, shelves, radiator, skirting boards.</li>
<li>Vacuum under the bed and behind furniture.</li>
<li>Wash the spare duvet, pillows and mattress protector if they've been in a cupboard, as well as the bedding.</li>
<li>Clean the window and sill, and the mirror.</li>
<li>Put out a set of clean towels and check the radiator works.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rooms that are kept closed and unheated can develop condensation and musty smells, especially in older terraces. Open the window and put the heating on in that room for a few days beforehand. If you spot black spots on walls or window frames, our guide to <a href="{{guide:damp-and-mould-in-older-homes}}">damp and black mould in older Hull homes</a> explains what to do.</p>
<h2>Kitchen and oven: getting ready for the big cook</h2>
<p>The kitchen works hardest over Christmas, so give it a proper clean a week or two ahead.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Oven:</strong> a dirty oven smokes when it's running hot for hours, and old grease can taint the food. Our guide on <a href="{{guide:how-to-clean-an-oven}}">how to clean an oven</a> compares DIY methods with booking <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">professional oven cleaning</a>, which uses fume-free, non-caustic products.</li>
<li><strong>Fridge and freezer:</strong> empty them before the big shop, bin anything out of date, wash the shelves and drawers and wipe the door seals.</li>
<li><strong>Hob and extractor:</strong> degrease the hob, wash the extractor filters and wipe the hood.</li>
<li><strong>Cupboards:</strong> clear space for extra food, serving dishes and glasses, and wipe the door fronts and handles.</li>
<li><strong>Microwave, kettle and toaster:</strong> clean inside the microwave, descale the kettle, empty the crumb tray.</li>
<li><strong>Worktops and bins:</strong> clear the worktops of anything you won't need and wash out the bins.</li>
</ul>
<p>For the full room, our <a href="{{guide:kitchen-deep-clean-checklist}}">kitchen deep clean checklist</a> covers every cupboard and appliance.</p>
<h2>Living room, dining room and hallway</h2>
<p>These are the rooms guests actually spend time in, and the hallway is the first thing they see.</p>
<h3>Hallway</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clean the front door inside and out, including the letterbox and handle.</li>
<li>Clear the coat hooks and shoe pile to make room for visitors' coats.</li>
<li>Wipe light switches, banisters and skirting boards, and vacuum the stairs.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Living and dining areas</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dust high surfaces, picture frames and shelves before the decorations go up; it's far harder afterwards.</li>
<li>Vacuum the sofa, under the cushions, and wash any throws and cushion covers.</li>
<li>Clean the TV screen, remotes and the coffee table.</li>
<li>Wash the dining table and chairs, and polish glasses that only come out once a year.</li>
</ul>
<p>Carpets and sofas look tired by December. If you book <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet and upholstery cleaning</a>, do it a few weeks ahead and check <a href="{{guide:how-long-carpets-take-to-dry}}">how long carpets take to dry</a> so the furniture is back in place well before Christmas.</p>
<h2>Bathroom checklist for overnight guests</h2>
<p>A clean bathroom matters more than anything else to people staying over.</p>
<ul>
<li>Descale the taps, shower head and shower screen.</li>
<li>Clean the toilet thoroughly, including the base and behind it.</li>
<li>Scrub the bath, sink and plugholes and wipe the tiles.</li>
<li>Put out clean towels, spare toilet rolls and hand soap.</li>
<li>Clear your own clutter from the windowsill and the side of the bath.</li>
<li>Empty the bin and wash the bath mat.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="{{guide:bathroom-deep-clean-guide}}">bathroom deep clean guide</a> covers grout, glass and limescale in detail.</p>
<h2>The one-hour reset before guests arrive</h2>
<p>With the deep cleaning done, the day before is just a quick refresh. Set a timer and work through this list:</p>
<ol>
<li>Clear the kitchen worktops and wipe them down.</li>
<li>Clean the toilet, sink and bathroom mirror, and put out fresh towels.</li>
<li>Empty every bin in the house.</li>
<li>Plump cushions and fold throws.</li>
<li>Vacuum the hallway, living room and stairs.</li>
<li>Mop the kitchen and hall floors.</li>
<li>Open the windows for ten minutes to air the house.</li>
</ol>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Put a basket by the door for shoes and a clear spot for coats. It keeps mud off the carpets and stops the hallway turning into an obstacle course.</p></div>
<h2>Get your home ready for Christmas without the stress</h2>
<p>December is a busy month for everyone, so if you'd like help, it's worth booking early. A <a href="{{service:one-off-cleaning}}">one-off pre-Christmas clean</a> covers the kitchen, bathrooms, living areas and guest rooms, carried out by vetted, insured cleaners who bring everything with them. We agree a fixed quote before we start, we're open seven days a week, and the same help is there for the clean-up after the festivities.</p>
<p>Our guide to <a href="{{guide:preparing-for-your-first-clean}}">preparing for your first professional clean</a> explains how to get the most from the visit. When you're ready, <a href="{{page:contact}}">contact our team</a> with your preferred dates.</p>
