	[
		'slug'        => 'bedroom-dust-mite-reduction',
		'title'       => 'Cleaning for Allergies and Asthma: Dust Mites, Pollen and Mould',
		'seo_title'   => 'Cleaning for Allergies & Asthma: Dust Mites and Mould',
		'description' => 'Cleaning for allergies and asthma: practical ways to cut dust mites, pollen, pet dander and mould at home, room by room, with a simple weekly routine.',
		'category'    => 'home-cleaning',
		'service'     => 'deep-cleaning',
		'related'     => [ 'damp-and-mould-in-older-homes', 'cleaning-with-pets', 'carpet-cleaning-methods-explained' ],
		'image'       => 'fresh clean bedroom',
		'excerpt'     => 'How to clean in a way that reduces dust mites, pollen, pet dander and mould spores at home, with an allergy-friendly cleaning schedule.',
		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>Cleaning for allergies and asthma means removing allergens, not just tidying: wash bedding hot every week, damp-dust instead of dry-dusting, vacuum with a HEPA-filtered machine and keep humidity down so dust mites and mould struggle to thrive. The aim is fewer particles on surfaces and in the air you breathe.</p>
<p>In Hull and East Yorkshire, damp older homes make mould a common trigger, while spring and summer bring pollen in from the surrounding countryside. The right routine helps with both. Cleaning won't cure allergies or replace medical advice, so speak to your GP or pharmacist about your symptoms, but it can make a real difference to how comfortable home feels.</p>
<p>A good starting point is a thorough reset: a <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> removes the dust that builds up in places a weekly clean doesn't reach, such as under beds, behind furniture and on top of wardrobes.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Bedding is the front line against dust mites: wash it weekly at 60°C if the fabric allows.</li><li>Damp-dust with microfibre and vacuum with a HEPA filter so allergens are captured, not stirred up.</li><li>Control moisture to control mould: ventilate, wipe condensation and avoid drying washing in closed rooms.</li><li>Avoid strong fragrances and aerosol sprays, which can irritate sensitive airways.</li></ul></div>
<h2>What triggers allergies at home?</h2>
<p>Most indoor allergy and asthma triggers fall into a handful of groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dust mites:</strong> tiny creatures that live in mattresses, pillows, carpets and soft furnishings. They thrive in warm, humid conditions.</li>
<li><strong>Pet dander:</strong> flakes of skin, saliva and urine proteins from cats, dogs and other pets. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-with-pets}}">cleaning a home with pets</a> covers hair and dander in detail.</li>
<li><strong>Mould spores:</strong> released by mould growing on damp walls, window frames, sealant and fabrics.</li>
<li><strong>Pollen:</strong> carried indoors on clothes, hair, pets and through open windows.</li>
<li><strong>Irritants:</strong> strong fragrances, aerosol sprays and bleach fumes, which aren't allergens but can set off symptoms.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How to reduce dust mites in the bedroom</h2>
<p>You spend hours each night close to your bedding, so the bedroom is where cleaning for allergies pays off most.</p>
<ul>
<li>Wash sheets and pillowcases weekly at 60°C, which is hot enough to kill dust mites, if the care label allows.</li>
<li>Use allergen-proof covers on the mattress, pillows and duvet, and wash them as directed.</li>
<li>Wash or replace pillows and duvets regularly, following the care label.</li>
<li>Vacuum the mattress when you change the bedding, using the upholstery tool.</li>
<li>Cut down on soft toys, cushions and throws, or wash them often. Putting soft toys in a sealed bag in the freezer overnight before washing is a common tip for items that can't take a hot wash.</li>
<li>Keep clutter off surfaces so dusting is quick and thorough.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Dusting and vacuuming without spreading allergens</h2>
<p>Done badly, dusting and vacuuming throw allergens back into the air. A few changes stop that happening.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Damp-dust with microfibre.</strong> A slightly damp cloth traps dust; a dry duster or feather duster scatters it.</li>
<li><strong>Work from top to bottom.</strong> Start with light fittings, curtain poles and the tops of wardrobes, finish with skirting boards and floors.</li>
<li><strong>Vacuum with a HEPA filter.</strong> A sealed machine with a HEPA filter holds on to fine particles. Empty it outdoors.</li>
<li><strong>Vacuum slowly and often.</strong> Slow passes pick up more, and two or three times a week is better than one big session.</li>
<li><strong>Clear the room.</strong> If possible, the person with allergies should be out of the room while you clean and for a while afterwards.</li>
</ol>
<p>Carpets and sofas hold allergens deep down, where a vacuum can't reach. Hot water extraction rinses them out, and our guide to <a href="{{guide:carpet-cleaning-methods-explained}}">hot water extraction vs dry carpet cleaning</a> explains the difference. We offer it through our <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet and upholstery cleaning service</a>.</p>
<h2>Keeping mould under control</h2>
<p>Mould needs moisture, so controlling damp is the most effective step. This is a familiar problem in the Victorian and Edwardian terraces around the Avenues and Holderness Road, where single glazing, solid walls and small bathrooms all add to condensation.</p>
<ul>
<li>Open windows or run extractor fans while cooking and showering, and for a while afterwards.</li>
<li>Wipe condensation from windows and sills each morning.</li>
<li>Dry laundry outdoors or in a vented tumble dryer where possible. If you dry indoors, use a room with the door shut and a window open or a dehumidifier running.</li>
<li>Keep furniture slightly away from external walls so air can circulate.</li>
<li>Clean small patches of mould with a suitable mould remover, wearing gloves and ideally a mask. Don't dry-brush mould, as it releases spores.</li>
</ul>
<p>Large areas of mould, or mould that keeps returning, need the cause fixing. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:damp-and-mould-in-older-homes}}">condensation, damp and black mould in older Hull homes</a> explains what to look for and when to involve your landlord or a specialist.</p>
<h2>Keeping pollen outside during hay fever season</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep windows closed on high-pollen days, particularly in the morning and early evening, and check the pollen forecast.</li>
<li>Change clothes and shower after spending time outdoors, so pollen doesn't end up on the sofa or pillow.</li>
<li>Damp-wipe windowsills and vacuum more often during the season.</li>
<li>Use a tumble dryer on high-pollen days, as washing dried outside collects pollen.</li>
<li>Wipe your pet's coat with a damp cloth when they come in.</li>
</ul>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Leave shoes and outdoor coats in the hallway. It stops pollen, dust and dirt being carried through the house, and it keeps carpets cleaner too.</p></div>
<h2>A cleaning schedule for allergies and asthma</h2>
<p>Regular, light cleaning keeps allergen levels lower than an occasional blitz. Fit these tasks into your <a href="{{guide:daily-cleaning-habits-busy-homes}}">weekly cleaning schedule</a>:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>How often</th><th>Why it helps</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wipe condensation from windows</td><td>Daily in colder months</td><td>Stops mould taking hold</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vacuum floors and sofas</td><td>Two or three times a week</td><td>Removes dust, dander and pollen</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wash bedding at 60°C</td><td>Weekly</td><td>Kills dust mites</td></tr>
<tr><td>Damp-dust surfaces</td><td>Weekly</td><td>Traps dust instead of spreading it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wash curtains, cushion covers and throws</td><td>Every few months</td><td>Clears allergens from soft furnishings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deep clean, including high and hidden areas</td><td>Every few months</td><td>Removes built-up dust a weekly clean misses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Professional carpet and upholstery clean</td><td>Once or twice a year</td><td>Lifts allergens from deep in the fibres</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For more on timing bigger cleans, see <a href="{{guide:when-to-book-a-deep-clean}}">how often you should deep clean your home</a>.</p>
<h2>Choosing products that won't make symptoms worse</h2>
<ul>
<li>Choose fragrance-free products where you can.</li>
<li>Spray onto a cloth rather than into the air, and avoid aerosols.</li>
<li>Ventilate the room while you clean and keep the person with asthma away from strong fumes.</li>
<li>Never mix products, especially bleach with acidic limescale removers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="{{guide:gentle-cleaning-hard-water-homes}}">eco-friendly cleaning products that actually work</a> covers gentler options, though "eco" doesn't always mean fragrance-free, so read the label.</p>
<h2>Getting help with an allergy-friendly deep clean</h2>
<p>If dusting high shelves or vacuuming mattresses sets off your symptoms, it makes sense to hand the job to someone else. Our <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep cleaning service</a> covers high and hidden areas, soft furnishings and every room, and eco-friendly products are available on request. Find out more about <a href="{{local:deep-cleaning-hull}}">deep cleaning in Hull</a>, and we also cover <a href="{{area:hessle}}">Hessle</a> and towns across East Yorkshire.</p>
<p>To keep allergens down afterwards, <a href="{{service:regular-house-cleaning}}">regular house cleaning</a> takes care of the weekly vacuuming and dusting. <a href="{{page:contact}}">Contact us</a> to talk through what you need and get a fixed quote.</p>
